---
/*
  One price card design, used by every page on the site that quotes a price.

  WHY THIS EXISTS

  On 2 September 2026 the site had six different price card designs. The same
  card, drawn six ways:

      homepage                      .pricing-card      house style, in style.css
      /services/web-design/         .wd-pricing-card   house style, copy 2
      /services/brand-identity/     .bp-pricing-card   house style, copy 3
      /services/video-production/   .vp-pricing-card   house style, copy 4
      /services/seo/                .seo-pricing-card  its own thing entirely
      /services/ai-automation/      .sp-card           its own thing entirely
      /services/digital-marketing/  .sp-card           the same again

  Four of those were the same CSS pasted under a different prefix, which is how
  the other two drifted without anyone noticing: there was no single place to
  change, so nobody changed all six. The SEO cards had a 24px corner where
  everything else had 12px, a sunken background where everything else was
  raised, no shadow, no hover, left aligned text and a green price. The AI
  Automation and Digital Marketing cards were smaller again, left aligned, and
  had no button on them at all, so the two newest services quoted a price and
  offered no way to act on it.

  Everything below is the design the homepage and three service pages already
  shared. Nothing here is invented; it is the majority design, written once.

  THE SERVICE HUE IS A VARIABLE, NOT A REDESIGN

  SEO is green and Brand Identity is purple across their whole pages, not just
  in pricing, and that is deliberate. So the accent is `--pc-accent`, set by the
  caller and defaulting to the site accent. Same card, same spacing, same
  structure, service hue on the badge, the ticks and the highlighted border.
  That turns a scattering of hex literals inside those two pricing sections into
  one value per page.

  TWO LISTS, ONE HIDDEN, AND WHY THE SERVER CANNOT CHOOSE

  Africa is priced from the cedi list and everyone else from the dollar list.
  Both are in the HTML and one carries `hidden`; the browser reveals the right
  one after asking /api/geo/, then converts its numbers into whatever the
  visitor's own country spends. The rule is written once in src/lib/markets.js
  and enforced by scripts/check-markets.mjs.

  The server cannot make this choice, because public pages are edge cached for
  60 seconds: whichever country the first visitor came from would be served to
  everyone for the next minute. International is the visible default, so a
  failed lookup shows dollars rather than showing cedis to someone who does not
  pay in cedis.

  EVERY PRICE SHIPS AS A NUMBER, NOT ONLY AS A STRING

  The CMS holds "from $3,000". Converting that needs the 3000 and the word
  "from" apart from each other, so parsePrice splits them into data-amount and
  data-prefix and the browser rebuilds the string in the visitor's currency.
  A tier with no number in it, "Custom", carries no data-amount and is therefore
  skipped by every conversion on the site and survives untouched.

  An empty priceUsd deliberately shows "Get a quote" rather than falling back to
  the cedi figure. GH₵ 2,500 read as dollars is about $425 next to a $3,000
  website on another live page, and the cheaper number wins.

  STYLES LIVE IN THIS FILE

  Astro scopes a component's style block to that component. Markup copied out of
  here arrives elsewhere carrying class names and no CSS, which is exactly how
  the service enquiry forms shipped looking broken on 31 August 2026.
*/
import { parsePrice, BASE_CURRENCY } from '../lib/markets.js';

interface Tier {
    name?: string;
    priceUsd?: string;
    price?: string;
    period?: string;
    description?: string;
    features?: { feature: string }[];
    isPopular?: boolean;
    /* A card that carries its own link and label, which the CMS pricing block
       lets an editor set per card. Falls back to the section's ctaHref. */
    href?: string;
    label?: string;
}

interface Props {
    tiers?: Tier[];
    heading?: string;
    subtitle?: string;
    note?: string;
    /* The service hue. A CSS colour, or nothing for the site accent. */
    accent?: string;
    /* The service hue as a heading gradient, when a page has one. Without it
       the heading keeps the site gradient from .text-gradient. */
    headingGradient?: string;
    /* Where a card's button goes. A string for every card, or a function of the
       tier when the destination carries the tier's name, as the SEO retainers
       do with their WhatsApp deep links. */
    ctaHref?: string | ((tier: Tier) => string);
    ctaLabel?: string;
    ctaExternal?: boolean;
    sectionId?: string;
    /*
      The dashed, sunken treatment for a one-off sitting in a row of retainers.

      The KICKER above the name is no longer behind this flag and is no longer
      optional: whether you pay once or every month is the most important fact
      on a price card, and until 4 September 2026 only the video page said it.
      Web design and brand identity said "starting at", which answers a
      different question, so nothing on those cards told you that GH₵ 5,000 was
      a single payment.

      This flag now controls the dashed border alone, which is a visual device
      that only helps where a cheap trial pack sits beside monthly plans.
    */
    markOneOff?: boolean;
}

const {
    tiers = [],
    heading,
    subtitle,
    note,
    accent,
    headingGradient,
    ctaHref = '/contact/',
    /*
      One label across a row.

      The service pages read "Get Started" on two cards and "Book a Call" on the
      third, three labels sitting side by side, which is the same inconsistency
      the homepage carried until 2 September 2026. Booking is also the honest
      verb: none of these buttons starts anything, every one leads to a form or
      a chat, and "Get Started" promises a checkout that does not exist.
    */
    ctaLabel = 'Book a call',
    ctaExternal = false,
    sectionId,
    markOneOff = false,
} = Astro.props as Props;

const hrefFor = (tier: Tier) => tier.href || (typeof ctaHref === 'function' ? ctaHref(tier) : ctaHref);
const labelFor = (tier: Tier) => tier.label || ctaLabel;

/*
  A one-off pack sitting next to a monthly plan is the single most misread thing
  on a price card: "GH₵ 1,800" and "GH₵ 1,800/mo" scan as the same kind of thing
  at a glance, and the difference between buying a batch once and paying every
  month is the most important fact on the card. Lifted from the video production
  page, which had solved this on its own.
*/
/* Both spellings are live in the CMS right now: video production writes
   "one-off \u00b7 3 reels" and AI Automation writes "one off". Matching only the
   hyphen would have left the AI Automation build looking like a subscription. */
const isOneOff = (period = '') => /^one[- ]off/i.test(period.trim());

/*
  Every spelling the CMS has ever held, mapped to one of three answers.

  "starting at" and "per project" are in here because they were the two values
  that produced no kicker at all, which is how the web design and brand identity
  cards ended up silent about whether their price recurred. Both describe a
  scoped piece of work delivered once, so both are one-off.

  A period this does not recognise returns nothing rather than guessing
  "Monthly", and scripts/check-markets.mjs fails the build when a priced tier
  produces no kicker, so an unrecognised value is caught before it ships rather
  than silently rendering a blank.
*/
const billingKicker = (period = '') => {
    const p = period.toLowerCase().trim();
    if (!p) return '';
    if (isOneOff(p)) return 'One-off';
    if (/^(starting at|per project|one time|once)\b/.test(p)) return 'One-off';
    if (p.includes('month') || /^\/?mo\b/.test(p)) return 'Monthly';
    if (p.includes('year') || /^\/?yr\b/.test(p)) return 'Yearly';
    return '';
};

/*
  What goes on the line under the amount.

  "starting at" was doing the kicker's job badly and the cadence's job not at
  all. Now the kicker says how often you pay and this says the same thing in
  the copy's own words, so "per project" and "one-off · 3 reels" both survive
  while the reader still gets an unambiguous One-off pill above the name.
*/
const cadenceLine = (period = '') => period.trim();

/*
  A price, split so the browser can rebuild it in another currency.

  `market` decides which of the tier's two figures is the base, and which
  currency that base is in. Returns the display string too, so the server render
  (which is what the edge cache holds, and what a visitor with JavaScript off
  keeps) is exactly what it was before any of this existed.
*/
const priceAttrs = (tier: Tier, market: 'africa' | 'international') => {
    const raw = market === 'africa'
        ? (tier.price || tier.priceUsd || '')
        : (tier.priceUsd || '');
    const display = raw || (market === 'africa' ? 'Get a quote' : 'Get a quote');
    const parsed = parsePrice(raw);
    /*
      "from", on every price that is a number, whoever typed it.

      The CMS held both spellings: nine cards said "from $3,000" and ten said
      "$3,000" for the same kind of promise. Forcing it here rather than fixing
      the ten strings means the eleventh cannot be typed wrong later, and the
      qualifier is true of every price on this site by policy: they are all
      floors that a real quote moves. A Custom tier has no number, so it gets
      nothing.
    */
    const prefix = parsed.amount === null ? '' : (parsed.prefix || 'from');
    const shown = parsed.amount === null
        ? display
        : (parsed.prefix ? display : `from ${display}`);
    return { display: shown, base: BASE_CURRENCY[market], amount: parsed.amount, prefix };
};

const hasTiers = tiers.length > 0;
const styleVar = accent ? `--pc-accent: ${accent}; --pc-ink: ${accent};` : undefined;

/* Written inline rather than as a class, because .text-gradient sets the
   `background` shorthand, and a class overriding it with an unset custom
   property would compute to `none` and render the heading invisible. */
const headingGradientStyle = headingGradient
    ? `background: ${headingGradient}; -webkit-background-clip: text; background-clip: text; -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;`
    : undefined;
---

{hasTiers && (
    <section class="section-padding pc-wrap" style={styleVar} id={sectionId}>
        <div class="container">
            {(heading || subtitle) && (
                <div class="section-header text-center animate-on-scroll slide-up">
                    {heading && (
                        <h2><span class="text-gradient" style={headingGradientStyle}>{heading}</span></h2>
                    )}
                    {subtitle && <p>{subtitle}</p>}
                    {/* Which currency these are in. Hidden until the country is
                        known, then filled in by initDynamicPricing. A currency
                        name baked into the HTML would be edge cached and served
                        to the wrong country. The four hand-built service pages
                        showed two currencies and named neither until
                        2 September 2026. */}
                    <p class="pc-currency" data-currency-note hidden></p>
                </div>
            )}

            <div class="pc-grid" data-count={tiers.length}>
                {tiers.map((tier, i) => (
                    <div class={`pc-card${tier.isPopular ? ' pc-popular' : ''}${markOneOff && isOneOff(tier.period) ? ' pc-oneoff' : ''} animate-on-scroll slide-up delay-${((i % 3) + 1) * 100}`}>
                        {tier.isPopular && <div class="pc-badge">Most Popular</div>}
                        {billingKicker(tier.period) && <span class="pc-kicker">{billingKicker(tier.period)}</span>}
                        <h3 class="pc-name">{tier.name}</h3>
                        {/* The dollar list, shown to everyone outside Africa and
                            converted into their own currency by the browser. */}
                        <div
                            class="pc-price"
                            data-market="international"
                            data-price
                            data-base={priceAttrs(tier, 'international').base}
                            data-amount={priceAttrs(tier, 'international').amount ?? undefined}
                            data-prefix={priceAttrs(tier, 'international').prefix || undefined}
                        >{priceAttrs(tier, 'international').display}</div>
                        {/* The cedi list, shown across Africa. Falls back to the
                            dollar figure rather than rendering empty: showMarket()
                            hides every international block and shows every africa
                            one, so a tier with no cedi price would otherwise show a
                            visitor in Accra no price at all. */}
                        <div
                            class="pc-price"
                            data-market="africa"
                            hidden
                            data-price
                            data-base={priceAttrs(tier, 'africa').base}
                            data-amount={priceAttrs(tier, 'africa').amount ?? undefined}
                            data-prefix={priceAttrs(tier, 'africa').prefix || undefined}
                        >{priceAttrs(tier, 'africa').display}</div>
                        <div class="pc-cycle">{cadenceLine(tier.period)}</div>
                        {tier.description && <p class="pc-desc">{tier.description}</p>}
                        {tier.features && tier.features.length > 0 && (
                            <ul class="pc-features">
                                {tier.features.map((f) => <li>{f.feature}</li>)}
                            </ul>
                        )}
                        <a
                            href={hrefFor(tier)}
                            target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                            rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                            class={`btn ${tier.isPopular ? 'btn-primary glow-effect' : 'btn-ghost'} btn-pill pc-btn`}
                        >{labelFor(tier)}</a>
                    </div>
                ))}
            </div>

            {note && <p class="pc-note">{note}</p>}
        </div>
    </section>
)}

<style>
    /*
      The service hue. A page sets one value and the badge, the ticks, the
      highlighted border and the button all follow.

      Two tokens rather than one, because they are not the same colour in light
      mode. --pc-accent fills a control, so it stays the bright brand blue.
      --pc-ink is the same hue used as *text* on a pale card, where the site
      darkens it to #0b6d9e to keep the contrast readable. A tick drawn in the
      fill colour is the light-mode bug this avoids.
    */
    .pc-wrap {
        --pc-accent: var(--accent);
        --pc-ink: var(--accent-text);
    }

    .pc-currency {
        font-family: var(--font-mono, monospace);
        font-size: 0.7rem;
        letter-spacing: 0.12em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        color: var(--text-subtle);
        margin: 14px 0 0;
    }

    .pc-grid {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
        gap: 32px;
        align-items: stretch;
    }
    @media (min-width: 992px) {
        .pc-grid[data-count="3"] { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
        .pc-grid[data-count="2"] { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; }
        .pc-grid[data-count="1"] { grid-template-columns: 1fr; max-width: 420px; margin: 0 auto; }
    }

    .pc-card {
        position: relative;
        background: var(--bg-surface);
        border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
        border-radius: var(--radius-md);
        padding: 48px 32px;
        text-align: center;
        transition: all 0.3s ease;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);

        /*
          A column, so the button can be pushed to the bottom.

          Cards in a row stretch to the tallest one, and the tiers carry between
          three and eight bullets, so the shorter cards ended with a button
          floating in the middle and a stretch of empty card beneath it.
        */
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
    }
    .pc-card:hover {
        transform: translateY(-5px);
        border-color: var(--border-strong);
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-md);
    }

    .pc-card.pc-popular {
        background: var(--bg-card);
        border-color: var(--pc-accent);
        transform: scale(1.05);
        padding: 64px 32px;
        z-index: 2;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-glow);
    }
    .pc-card.pc-popular:hover {
        transform: scale(1.05) translateY(-5px);
        border-color: var(--pc-accent);
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-glow);
    }

    /* A dashed edge and a sunken surface stop a one-off pack reading as "the
       cheap monthly plan" beside a retainer. */
    .pc-card.pc-oneoff {
        border-style: dashed;
        background: var(--bg-inset);
    }

    /*
      Unless the one-off IS the flagship, which it is on AI Automation, where
      the build is both the popular tier and a single payment. Dashing the
      highlighted card outlines it like an unfinished placeholder, and the two
      treatments then say opposite things about the same tier. The kicker above
      the name still reads "One-off", so nothing is lost by letting the
      highlight win the border.
    */
    .pc-card.pc-oneoff.pc-popular {
        border-style: solid;
        background: var(--bg-card);
    }

    .pc-badge {
        position: absolute;
        top: 0;
        left: 50%;
        transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
        background: var(--pc-accent);
        color: #000;  /* theme-exempt: dark text on an accent-filled control; must not invert */
        font-size: 12px;
        font-weight: 700;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 1px;
        padding: 6px 16px;
        border-radius: 50px;
        white-space: nowrap;
    }

    .pc-kicker {
        align-self: center;
        font-size: 11px;
        font-weight: 700;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 1px;
        color: var(--text-muted);
        border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
        border-radius: 50px;
        padding: 4px 12px;
        margin-bottom: 12px;
    }
    .pc-card.pc-oneoff .pc-kicker {
        color: var(--pc-ink);
        border-color: var(--pc-ink);
    }

    .pc-name {
        font-size: 24px;
        margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    /*
      One line, always.

      At a fixed 40px "from $2,500/mo" did not fit a three-up card and broke
      after "from", so the word rendered alone on its own line above the number.
      The prices are "from" prices now rather than bare figures, so the string is
      longer than that size was chosen for.
    */
    .pc-price {
        font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 2.6vw, 2.5rem);
        font-family: var(--font-heading);
        font-weight: 700;
        color: var(--text-primary);
        line-height: 1.15;
        margin-bottom: 4px;
        white-space: nowrap;
    }

    /*
      How often you pay. Quiet, directly under the amount, and always occupying
      its line, so "one off" and "a month" sit in the same place on every card
      and a reader can tell a website apart from a subscription without reading
      a word of the body.
    */
    .pc-cycle {
        font-size: 14px;
        color: var(--text-subtle);
        letter-spacing: 0.04em;
        margin-bottom: 16px;
        min-height: 1.2em;
    }

    .pc-desc {
        font-size: 14px;
        color: var(--text-muted);
        line-height: 1.55;
        margin-bottom: 32px;
        /* Descriptions run to one line or two, so a floor keeps every feature
           list in a row starting on the same line. */
        min-height: 42px;
    }

    .pc-features {
        list-style: none;
        padding: 0;
        margin: 0 0 40px;
        text-align: left;
        /* Takes the slack, so the button below it lands at the foot of the card. */
        flex: 1 0 auto;
    }
    .pc-features li {
        position: relative;
        font-size: 15px;
        line-height: 1.45;
        margin-bottom: 16px;
        padding-left: 28px;
        color: var(--text-muted);
    }
    .pc-features li::before {
        content: '\2713';
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        top: 0;
        color: var(--pc-ink);
        font-weight: bold;
    }

    .pc-btn {
        width: 100%;
        /* Pushes the button to the foot of the card, so every button in a row
           lands on the same line whether the card has eight bullets or none. */
        margin-top: auto;
    }
    /* The highlighted tier's button takes the service hue rather than the site
       accent, which is what the inline styles on the SEO and Brand Identity
       pages were doing by hand. */
    .pc-card.pc-popular .pc-btn.btn-primary {
        background: var(--pc-accent);
        border-color: var(--pc-accent);
    }

    .pc-note {
        max-width: 720px;
        margin: 32px auto 0;
        text-align: center;
        font-size: 14px;
        line-height: 1.55;
        color: var(--text-muted);
    }

    /*
      Four up.

      Each card is about 265px wide at this count, where the three-up padding
      and price size no longer fit, and the highlighted card's scale() would eat
      the gap now that it has neighbours on both sides.
    */
    @media (min-width: 992px) {
        .pc-grid[data-count="4"] { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 20px; }
        .pc-grid[data-count="4"] .pc-card { padding: 40px 22px; }
        .pc-grid[data-count="4"] .pc-card.pc-popular {
            transform: none;
            padding: 40px 22px;
        }
        .pc-grid[data-count="4"] .pc-card.pc-popular:hover { transform: translateY(-5px); }
        .pc-grid[data-count="4"] .pc-price { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2vw, 1.9rem); }
    }

    /* Below the breakpoint nothing is side by side, so the highlighted card has
       no neighbours to stand out from and scaling it only clips its badge. */
    @media (max-width: 991px) {
        .pc-card.pc-popular {
            transform: none;
            padding: 48px 32px;
        }
        .pc-card.pc-popular:hover { transform: translateY(-5px); }
    }

    /* The same two steps the homepage cards take, so a phone shows one card
       design and not two. */
    @media (max-width: 480px) {
        .pc-card, .pc-card.pc-popular { padding: 32px 20px; }
    }
    @media (max-width: 360px) {
        .pc-card, .pc-card.pc-popular { padding: 28px 16px; }
    }

    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .pc-card { transition: none; }
        .pc-card:hover, .pc-card.pc-popular:hover { transform: none; }
    }
</style>
